SESSION recovery does not require reconstructing historical runner/helper
state from a commit or stash. Submitted old authoritative blocks, not old
whole files, are verified against the persisted terminal fingerprint.
The block spans the current unique trace node's `@id` line start through
the target test statement end. Splice the old block into that boundary of
the current file, preserve its trace identity, and verify it with the same
`testFingerprintFromText(currentNode, splicedText)` algorithm.
Old/new block variants run as a synthetic pair on the same current
content-addressed declared file/dependency/production input snapshot. The pair manifest
proves byte-identical content outside the block, including top-level
hooks/mocks/imports/helpers; required or present differences outside it
reject test-only, even if the block fingerprint matches.
Non-unique nodes/paths or nondeterministic boundaries reject without writes.
DES-M5-023 declares globalSetup's dist rebuild and mutable caches/temp/reports
as outputs, not immutable inputs. Both independently rebuilt dist inventories
and their post-run inventories must match by path/mode/raw digest; copied
installed dependencies suffice without installs, fetches or tarball caches.
The guarantee is separate roots, sanitized environment and before/after
declared input/output manifests, not OS filesystem/network confinement.
Both fresh selected-only reports and all input/output digests are fixed for semantic
review and exact-hash approval. The splice-only SESSION clock refactor is an
acceptance fixture, while outside-block hook/mock changes are rejection
fixtures. Failure to reconstruct an old block does not authorize a fabricated
Red; behavior changes still require genuine observed Red-to-Green evidence.
This proves the reviewed test-only change on that snapshot, not a replay of
the historical Refactor 3317 execution.

